  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an array AAA of length NNN.\n\nYou have to partition the elements of the array into some subsequences such that:\n\n\nEach element AiA_iAi​ (1≤i≤N)(1 \\le i \\le N)(1≤i≤N) belongs to **exactly one** subsequence.\nThe mean of the mean of subsequences is **maximised**.\nFormally, let S1,S2,…,SKS_1, S_2, \\dots, S_KS1​,S2​,…,SK​ denote KKK subsequences of array AAA such that each element AiA_iAi​ (1≤i≤N)(1 \\le i \\le N)(1≤i≤N) belongs to exactly one subsequence SjS_jSj​ (1≤j≤K)(1 \\le j \\le K)(1≤j≤K).\n\nLet XjX_jXj​ (1≤j≤K)(1 \\le j \\le K)(1≤j≤K) denote the mean of the elements of subsequence SjS_jSj​. You need to maximise the value ∑j=1KXjK\\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^K X_j}{K}K∑j=1K​Xj​​.\n\nPrint the maximum value. The answer is considered correct if the relative error is less than 10−610^{-6}10−6.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nFirst line will contain TTT, number of test cases. Then the test cases follow.\nFirst line of each test case consists of a single integer NNN - denoting the length of array AAA.\nSecond line of each test case consists of NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​ - denoting the array AAA.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output in a single line, the **maximum** possible mean of mean of subsequences. The answer is considered correct if the relative error is less than 10−610^{-6}10−6.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤10001 \\leq T \\leq 10001≤T≤1000",
      "2≤N≤1052 \\leq N \\leq 10^52≤N≤105",
      "1≤Ai≤1061 \\leq A_i \\leq 10^61≤Ai​≤106",
      "Sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 3⋅1053 \\cdot 10^53⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n2\n10 20\n3\n1 2 3\n5\n50 50 50 50 50",
        "output": "15\n2.25\n50",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 111: We can partition the array in 222 ways - ({10},{20})(\\{10\\}, \\{20\\})({10},{20}) or ({10,20})(\\{10,20\\})({10,20}). In both cases, mean of mean of subsequences comes out to be 151515.\nTest Case 222: The optimal partition is ({1,2},{3})(\\{1, 2\\}, \\{3\\})({1,2},{3}).\n\nMean of first subsequence =1+22=1.5 = \\frac{1+2}{2} = 1.5=21+2​=1.5.\nMean of second subsequence =31=3 = \\frac{3}{1} = 3=13​=3.\n\nThus, mean of mean of subsequences is 1.5+32=4.52=2.25\\frac{1.5+3}{2} = \\frac{4.5}{2} = 2.2521.5+3​=24.5​=2.25.\nTest Case 333: Any partition will yield the mean of mean of subsequences as 505050."
      }
    ]
  },
